You're encountering a common issue when displaying content from APIs that provide text with Markdown formatting directly into an HTML page. HTML browsers don't inherently understand Markdown syntax (like `**bold**`, `*italic*`, ````code``` `). They treat it as plain text.

To prevent the raw Markdown from showing and instead display it as properly formatted HTML, you need to **convert the Markdown string to HTML** before placing it into your HTML element.

Here are the primary ways to do this, focusing on client-side JavaScript as you mentioned "basic JS":

---

### Option 1: Client-Side Markdown-to-HTML Library (Recommended for "basic JS")

This is the most straightforward approach. You use a JavaScript library that takes a Markdown string and outputs an HTML string.

1.  **Include a Markdown Parser Library:**
    The most popular and widely used libraries for this are `marked.js`, `showdown.js`, or `markdown-it`. `marked.js` is a great choice for its simplicity and robustness.

    You can include it via a CDN in your HTML file:

    ```html
    <!DOCTYPE html>
    <html lang="en">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
        <title>Markdown to HTML</title>
        <!-- Include marked.js from a CDN -->
        <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/marked/marked.min.js"></script>
        <style>
            body { font-family: sans-serif; margin: 20px; }
            #output {
                border: 1px solid #ccc;
                padding: 15px;
                background-color: #f9f9f9;
                max-width: 800px;
                margin-top: 20px;
            }
            /* Basic styling for markdown elements */
            #output h1, #output h2, #output h3 { color: #333; }
            #output p { margin-bottom: 1em; line-height: 1.6; }
            #output ul, #output ol { margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 1em; }
            #output pre {
                background-color: #eee;
                padding: 10px;
                border-radius: 4px;
                overflow-x: auto;
            }
            #output code {
                font-family: monospace;
                background-color: #e0e0e0;
                padding: 2px 4px;
                border-radius: 3px;
            }
        </style>
    </head>
    <body>
        <h1>API Response Display</h1>
        <div id="output">
            Loading...
        </div>

        <script>
            document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', async () => {
                const outputDiv = document.getElementById('output');

                try {
                    // Simulate fetching data from your API
                    // Replace this with your actual API call
                    const response = await fetch('YOUR_API_ENDPOINT'); // e.g., 'https://api.example.com/chatbot-response'
                    const data = await response.json(); // Assuming your API returns JSON

                    // Let's assume the Markdown content is in a 'text' property of the JSON
                    const markdownText = data.text || "No content found from API.";

                    // 1. Convert Markdown to HTML using marked.js
                    const htmlContent = marked.parse(markdownText);

                    // 2. Set the innerHTML of your target div
                    outputDiv.innerHTML = htmlContent;

                } catch (error) {
                    console.error('Error fetching or processing data:', error);
                    outputDiv.innerHTML = `<p style="color: red;">Failed to load content: ${error.message}</p>`;
                }
            });

            // Example of how you'd get Markdown content if simulating
            // For testing without an actual API:
            // const simulatedApiResponse = {
            //     text: "# Welcome!\n\nThis is a paragraph with **bold text** and *italic text*.\n\n- Item one\n- Item two\n\n```javascript\nconsole.log('Hello from code block');\n```"
            // };
            // Then you'd use `simulatedApiResponse.text` instead of `data.text`
        </script>
    </body>
    </html>
    ```

2.  **Key Steps in your JavaScript:**
    *   Fetch your API response as usual.
    *   Get the string containing the Markdown from the response (e.g., `data.yourMarkdownProperty`).
    *   Call the parser function (e.g., `marked.parse(markdownString)`) to get the HTML string.
    *   Set the `innerHTML` property of your target HTML element (e.g., `document.getElementById('output').innerHTML = htmlString;`). **Do NOT use `textContent`**, as that would display the HTML tags as raw text again.

#### Security Note (XSS):
When using `innerHTML`, there's always a potential for Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) if the content you're displaying is untrusted. Markdown parser libraries like `marked.js` generally include built-in sanitization to remove malicious scripts. `marked.js` has a `sanitize` option which is `true` by default in newer versions, providing good protection. Always ensure you are using a reputable and up-to-date library.

---

### Option 2: Server-Side Markdown-to-HTML Conversion (If you have a backend)

If your "basic JS" is part of a larger application with a backend (e.g., Node.js, Python, PHP), you could perform the Markdown conversion on the server before sending it to the client.

1.  **Backend receives Markdown:** Your server-side code fetches the Markdown content from the LLM API.
2.  **Convert on server:** Use a server-side Markdown library (e.g., `markdown-it` for Node.js, `markdown` for Python, `Parsedown` for PHP) to convert the Markdown string to HTML.
3.  **Send HTML to client:** Your server then sends the *already converted HTML* string to your client-side JavaScript.
4.  **Display on client:** Your client-side JavaScript simply places this HTML string directly into `innerHTML`.

This approach keeps your client-side code lighter and can be beneficial for larger applications or if you need more control over the rendering process.

---

### Additional Considerations:

*   **Styling:** The converted HTML will need CSS to look good. Markdown converters output standard HTML elements (like `<h1>`, `<p>`, `<ul>`, `<pre>`, `<code>`). You'll want to add CSS rules to style these elements within your `#output` div. I've added some basic CSS in the example above.
*   **Syntax Highlighting for Code Blocks:** If your API responses often include code blocks (` ```language `), you might want to add a client-side syntax highlighter like `highlight.js` or `Prism.js`. These libraries work by scanning your HTML for `<code>` blocks and applying styling. You would include them *after* `marked.js` and call their initialization function after `innerHTML` is set.
*   **Error Handling:** Always include `try...catch` blocks for your API calls to gracefully handle network issues or API errors.

For your scenario with "basic JS", **Option 1 (client-side library like `marked.js`)** is almost certainly the way to go.
